From: Magnus Reimann <magnus.reimann@mailbox.org>
Subject: [PATCH netdde] libddekit: do not sleep forever on a timer due within 1ms

The timer thread computes how long to sleep until the first timer as

    to = (timer_list->expires - jiffies) * 1000000 / HZ;
    to /= 1000;

and passes it to ddekit_sem_down_timed, for which a timeout of 0 means
waiting without a timeout.  When the first timer is due in less than a
millisecond, or already due (the unsigned difference then wraps), the
timer thread can thus block forever with a pending timer, and no timer
of the process ever runs again.

This was seen on an SMP gnumach (all processors in the default
processor set) as a sporadic boot hang of Debian GNU/Hurd amd64:
netdde's only pending timer was e1000_watchdog (expires 225, id 2),
the ddekit timer thread was in ddekit_sem_down_timed (timo=0), the
e1000 link thus never came up (dev->state NOCARRIER, no packet ever
sent), pfinet's timer thread was blocked in device_write while holding
global_lock, and dhclient hung in socket_send.  With real parallelism
the timer is more likely to be added right when the timer thread
recomputes its timeout.

Compute the difference as a signed number of jiffies, run due timers
without sleeping, and round sub-millisecond waits up to 1ms.


Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/libddekit/timer.c
+++ b/libddekit/timer.c
@@ -289,16 +289,24 @@
 		unsigned long  to     = DDEKIT_TIMEOUT_NEVER;
 
 		if (timer_list) {
+			long jdiff = (long) (timer_list->expires - jiffies);
 #if 0
-			int jdiff = timer_list->expires - jiffies;
 			ddekit_printf("\033[31mscheduling new timeout.\033[0m\n");
 			ddekit_printf("\033[31mjiffies diff = %ld (%d s)\033[0m\n", jdiff, jdiff/HZ);
 #endif
-			to = (timer_list->expires - jiffies) * 1000000 / HZ;
-			to /= 1000;
+			if (jdiff <= 0)
+				/* Already due: run it without sleeping.  */
+				to = 0;
+			else {
+				to = jdiff * 1000 / HZ;
+				/* A timeout of 0 would mean sleeping forever.  */
+				if (to == 0)
+					to = 1;
+			}
 		}
 
-		__timer_sleep(to);
+		if (to)
+			__timer_sleep(to);
 
 		while ((timer = get_next_timer()) != NULL) {
 			ddekit_lock_unlock (&timer_lock);
